And it wasn't even in the beginning of February, it was towards the middle of it.

162
00:10:14,723 --> 00:10:18,004
This tells you how many inbound interactions.

163
00:10:18,364 --> 00:10:23,105
That means how many people have called Care Solace for assistance, how many calls there were.

164
00:10:24,205 --> 00:10:25,685
But these are what we wanna focus on.

165
00:10:25,785 --> 00:10:33,287
So the warm handoffs are how many people have been referred personally by Medford Public Schools staff, how many were done.

166
00:10:33,347 --> 00:10:40,549
So we had 43 in February, 45 in March, 37 in April, and one so far in May.

167
00:10:40,909 --> 00:10:42,109
Of course, we haven't gotten that far.

168
00:10:43,409 --> 00:10:46,611
And here is the key point here.

169
00:10:46,771 --> 00:10:49,553
How many actual appointments into care were made?

170
00:10:50,513 --> 00:10:55,996
So out of the 43 in February, seven appointments were made in February.

171
00:10:56,056 --> 00:10:59,338
So some of these carry over and so on.

172
00:10:59,478 --> 00:11:04,781
But if you notice, we've done 136 warm handoffs and only 40 appointments were made.

173
00:11:05,461 --> 00:11:08,463
And this is indicative of a problem which is twofold.

174
00:11:09,143 --> 00:11:18,750
One is people will agree to a warm handoff for us to say, look, we're going to, we're going to do, you know, we can have people who can search for a therapist for you to assist you in the community.

175
00:11:19,250 --> 00:11:20,271
They'll say, that's great.

176
00:11:20,391 --> 00:11:26,575
And either they don't respond to calls from Care Solace, or they aren't actually interested in making that appointment.

177
00:11:27,636 --> 00:11:31,399
The other challenge that they have is they may do a search on their own.

178
00:11:31,879 --> 00:11:40,884
and say, you know what, I'm going to go through my pediatrician's office and decline services through Care Solace, or Care Solace is still working on trying to find a provider.

179
00:11:41,584 --> 00:11:42,545
So it's great.

180
00:11:42,645 --> 00:11:43,465
It's assisting us.

181
00:11:43,705 --> 00:11:44,346
I love them.

182
00:11:44,666 --> 00:11:48,108
But the challenge is they're pulling from a dry well, like all of us.

183
00:11:48,188 --> 00:11:49,949
There just isn't the care out there.

184
00:11:50,609 --> 00:11:57,553
I'll walk you through a little bit more as far as what these indicators show us, who's being referred, because I think it tells us a good story here.

185
00:11:59,179 --> 00:12:10,805
So if you look at the gender breakdown, 63% of females, 34 male, 3% non-binary, I think that's a typical breakdown of what we're looking for.

186
00:12:10,845 --> 00:12:18,670
I think in our society, we're still looking at a situation where females are more apt to seek care, mental health care.

187
00:12:19,971 --> 00:12:25,994
But in terms of age groups who we've referred so far, 28% of our referrals have been for adults.

188
00:12:27,262 --> 00:12:33,445
So either adults within our school or caregivers for our students, parents, caregivers, grandparents.

189
00:12:34,706 --> 00:12:41,790
The largest group, which is no surprise to me, though I expected it to be larger, is 45% have been adolescents 12 to 17.

190
00:12:42,431 --> 00:12:47,914
And then 27%, which I was surprised about, that's a higher group of three to 11.

191
00:12:48,794 --> 00:12:51,596
So those are our littlest learners here.

192
00:12:54,057 --> 00:12:54,958
And then they talk about,

193
